Explain how a web application connects to a hosted database

The README told you how to drive the local binary by piping into its
REPL, and how the hosted service is built, but never how to put the two
together: what a web application actually calls, and what to hand a
developer starting one.

The section leads with the constraint that decides the architecture. The
instance token is the entire authorisation - it reads, writes, deletes
and truncates - so it cannot go to a browser, and the platform enforces
that rather than trusting anyone to remember it: the instance API sends
no CORS headers at all, so a browser refuses the call before it leaves.
The only shape is browser to your server to asmdb.

It then gives the four facts a coding agent needs, the route table, and
the record shape - which matters more than the routes, because one fixed
record and one table per database decides how the application models its
data before a line is written. Integers travel as strings, since a u64
id does not survive a JavaScript number.

I nearly published that over-long tags and content are truncated. They
are rejected: parse.inc jumps to .toolong rather than trimming. Checking
turned a helpful-sounding sentence into a correct one, which is the
difference between a reader validating input and a reader discovering
that a row never landed.

